// T ML340 530,531,532,533,534,535,536,537,538,539,540,541,542,543,544 | The Muslim Lantern | dash: | cut:0
#host("The Muslim Lantern", ("Sorry you had the question I know yes? OK, Excellent, so, because Eid Al Adha is very soon, they're not too far from each other, From Eid Al Fitr, it's going to be in June; 26 or 24 27th of June, it's very close basically, Eid Al Fitr the word “فطر” means to \"break fast\" literally, It is the holiday of breaking the period of fasting, the 30 days that you've done, thanking God for the blessings, That he allowed you to live, and all of that, and also Eid it's a very important thing; because there's obligatory charity to be given in Eid Al Fitr, A specific amount of material things that you need to give to the poor, everyone has to give it, So there is Eid Al Fitr \"because we breaking in the fast\", and Eid Al Adha means to sacrifice “أضحية” is a sacrifice, So Adha Eid it's a celebration of sacrifice, and that tradition traces all the way back to Abraham,", "If you're aware of the story of Abraham and his two sons and God told him not to sacrifice his son, And then God sent him a lamb, a ram or a lamb whatever you want to say, and then he sacrificed it, That tradition we kind of imitate that tradition in a way as a form of gratitude to the Creator, And also we distribute the meat, a portion of the meat every Muslim has to distribute it, we feed the poor people, So these are the two significance between these holidays, and also it's linked to Hajj, Because it comes after Hajj, which’s pilgrimage, it’s after pilgrimage, So, it's also finishing the period of pilgrimage, now there's a celebration of Eid, OK, yes?",))
#source("Source: The Muslim Lantern, \"Non-Muslim Audience Asks Muslim Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali\"")
#sep()
